d a patient’s DNA at the molecular level and identify the specific mutations driving their disease. Using this highly specific information, we design a synthetic mRNA sequence that encodes instructions for cells to produce proteins mimicking the disease markers—like those produced by cancer cells or viruses.\n\nThis mRNA is synthesized in a lab using a DNA template and advanced enzymes, purified, and packaged for delivery into the patient’s body.\n\nOnce inside the patient’s cells, the mRNA reaches the ribosomes, the cell’s protein factories, which read the instructions like a blueprint. The ribosomes assemble amino acids into a protein that mimics a marker from the disease, such as cancer antigens or viral spike proteins. The affected cells then process and put these proteins on full display, acting as red flags for the immune system to identify and attack—unlike disease-related proteins, which are often hidden or suppressed to evade detection.\n\nWhen the immune system recognizes these proteins as threats, it activates T cells to destroy similar markers on actual disease cells and produces antibodies to prevent future attacks, effectively teaching the body how to fight the disease.\n\nIn other words, mRNA can be personalized to transform a patient’s own immune system into a precision weapon, honing in specifically on the target disease. Not only is this effective on an individual basis, but it also enables the patient’s body to develop its own treatment. With just a blood test and a shot!\n\nBeyond cancer, mRNA holds the potential to tackle challenges like infectious diseases, genetic disorders, and even autoimmune conditions, revolutionizing the future of healthcare.', 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 2, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Wed Jan 22 14:11:01 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1882051251686297890', 'display_text_range': [14, 294], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbol